    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n10658" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">con-sŭādĕo</span></b> ,
			ēre, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>v. a., to advise</i> or <i>counsel strongly</i> (Plautin.; cf. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">however, consuasor): id consuadeo,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut. Merc. 1, 2, 32</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">consuadet homini,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Trin. 2, 4, 126</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">qui consuadet, vetat,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. ib. 3, 2, 46</span></i>
			.—Of favorable auspices: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">picus et cornix ab laevā consuadent,
					etc.,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut. As. 2, 1, 13</span></i>
			.</div>
